  "title": "Chapter 17 – Ohm’s Law and Electrical Measurements",
  "order": 15,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "ap2btzm",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Introduction"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Ohm's Law establishes that potential difference (V) is directly proportional to electric current (I) at constant temperature.",
        "Resistance (R) measures opposition to current flow and depends on conductor length, area, material resistivity, and temperature.",
        "Ammeters are low-resistance instruments connected in series to measure circuit current.",
        "Voltmeters are high-resistance instruments connected in parallel to measure potential difference across components.",
        "Electrical power consumed in a circuit is given by P = VI = I²R = V²/R and is measured in Watts."
      ],
      "id": "navdn2x"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Primary Formula",
          "value": "V = IR",
          "icon": "book"
        },
        {
          "label": "Unit of Resistance",
          "value": "Ohm (Ω)",
          "icon": "award"
        },
        {
          "label": "Ammeter Placement",
          "value": "Series",
          "icon": "check"
        },
        {
          "label": "Voltmeter Placement",
          "value": "Parallel",
          "icon": "info"
        }
      ],
      "id": "q2h943k"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7rrxi2w",
      "content": "Ohm’s Law is one of the most fundamental principles in electrical engineering. It explains the relationship between voltage, current, and resistance in an electrical circuit. Understanding Ohm’s Law helps technicians calculate electrical quantities and troubleshoot circuits."
    },
    {
      "id": "1gebh9l",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Ohm’s Law"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wi84blv",
      "content": "Ohm’s Law states that the current flowing through a conductor is directly proportional to the voltage applied across it and inversely proportional to the resistance of the conductor, provided temperature remains constant."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3ftjdsv",
      "content": "Mathematical Formula:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "2ofsr2o",
      "content": "V = I × R"
    },
    {
      "id": "sbzuiat",
